        "math_academy_id": "q-65293",
        "problem": "If $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}=\\frac{1}{f(x)},$ then $f(x)=$",
        "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the denominator using $1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x={sin}^{2}\u2061x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}{{sin}^{2}\u2061x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the denominator as difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}{1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}{(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+1}}{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)}(1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=1-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x.$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
